Example 1 – simple multiplication
  Calculate &frac{2}{5} × &frac{3}{4}
  Multiply numerators: 2 × 3 = 6
  Multiply denominators: 5 × 4 = 20
  &frac{6}{20} = &frac{3}{10}
  = &frac{3}{10}
  (Simplify by dividing top and bottom by 2.)

Example 2 – cancelling first
  Calculate &frac{3}{8} × &frac{4}{9}
  Cancel: 3 and 9 share factor 3; 4 and 8 share factor 4
  &frac{1}{2} × &frac{1}{3}
  = &frac{1}{6}
  = &frac{1}{6}
  (Cross-cancel before multiplying keeps numbers small.)

Calculate. Give answers in simplest form.

(a) &frac{2}{7} × &frac{7}{8}
(b) 4 × &frac{3}{8}
(c) &frac{9}{16} × &frac{4}{3}
(d) 2&frac{1}{2} × 1&frac{3}{5}
(e) &frac{5}{6} × &frac{2}{5} × &frac{3}{4}

✓ &frac{2}{3} × &frac{4}{5} = &frac{8}{15}
✓ Multiply tops and multiply bottoms
✗ &frac{2}{3} × &frac{4}{5} = &frac{6}{8}
✗ You need a common denominator to multiply
✓ Cross-cancel before multiplying to simplify

